# CARELENS — MASTER ENGINEERING, RELIABILITY & TECHNICAL EXCELLENCE DIRECTIVE

## YOUR ROLE

Act as a principal software engineer, computer-vision engineer, AI reliability researcher, security engineer, accessibility specialist, and demanding code reviewer working on my existing application, CareLens.

Your job is to transform CareLens into an exceptionally well-engineered, evidence-grounded, dependable, accessible, and technically sophisticated home-safety assessment application for older adults, people with mobility limitations, family caregivers, and other users.

This is an implementation task, not a brainstorming exercise.

You have permission to inspect, refactor, improve, test, and extend the existing codebase as necessary to accomplish this objective. Preserve the product's useful functionality and visual identity while substantially improving the quality of its underlying implementation.

The target is not merely a polished frontend that calls an AI API. The target is a coherent application with reliable processing, defensible findings, transparent reasoning, robust failure handling, measurable performance, and maintainable engineering.

## THE MOST IMPORTANT RULE: INSPECT, EVALUATE, THEN ACT

Before changing anything, inspect the actual repository and determine how the application currently works.

Do not blindly assume that a feature is missing because it appears in this specification. Do not blindly assume that it is implemented correctly because a component, function, button, or similarly named file exists.

For EVERY major requirement, make one of these decisions:

**A. Already implemented excellently:** Verify the actual behavior in code and through appropriate tests. If it meets the requirement thoroughly, preserve it and move to the next requirement.

**B. Implemented but improvable:** Improve the existing implementation in place. Preserve sound architecture and working behavior. Fix weaknesses rather than adding a duplicate implementation.

**C. Partially implemented or unreliable:** Finish the existing feature, correct its logic, integrate it properly, and test it.

**D. Missing:** Implement the feature using the existing technology stack and architecture wherever practical.

**E. Infeasible, unnecessary, or unsafe:** Explain the specific reason, implement the strongest reasonable alternative when possible, and do not pretend the original requirement was completed.

A feature is not complete merely because its UI exists. It must behave correctly, handle relevant edge cases, and be integrated into the real application.

Do not waste time rebuilding excellent functionality. Do not skip mediocre functionality. Do not create parallel systems that compete with existing ones.

Inspect the real source code, dependencies, configuration, API boundaries, database schema, authentication, storage, routing, state management, and tests before deciding what to change.

Do not assume any filename, service, API, database, or architecture described below exists until you verify it.

